---
// /about — the answer-shaped page.
//
// This exists for a specific, narrow reason documented in docs/designs/llm-discovery-marketing.md §5.2
// and §4.3: when someone asks an assistant "is there an audio tour app for driving around Lake
// Tahoe?", the answer is synthesised from pages, and the ONLY non-vendor study of what earns a
// citation (Aggarwal et al., GEO, KDD 2024) found the winning shape is CITED, QUANTIFIED, honest
// prose — while keyword stuffing scored below baseline. So this page answers real questions with real
// numbers and links its sources, and does nothing else.
//
// ⚠ It is deliberately NOT a comparison page. docs/research/autio-content-moat.md is a founder-blessed
// DON'T on fighting Autio/Shaka on the breadth grid: in a comparison table breadth wins and charm has
// no cell. Naming competitors here would drag us onto exactly that grid.
//
// ⚠ NOT the shelved programmatic corpus→web build (§5.3). That was declined on the merits: our facts
// are downstream of Wikipedia, which is the single most-cited source in the answer surface, so
// restating it in a worse-attributed CC BY-SA-encumbered form competes with the page models already
// prefer. This page is about the PRODUCT, which Wikipedia has no article on.
//
// Distinct from /support on purpose: that page troubleshoots for people who already installed it.
// This one answers the questions asked BEFORE installing.
//
// ⚠ Copy calls made in the 2026-08-20 pre-release sweep (founder): coverage is "Lake Tahoe", no
// state — the basin spans California AND Nevada, so naming one was wrong for the east shore; the
// stop count reads "hundreds" rather than a number, deliberately, so it can't drift stale. The
// "What do I need?" answer flips on APP_STORE_URL with the rest of the go-live copy.
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro'
import { APP_STORE_URL } from '../appStore'
---

<Legal
  heading="What Skipper is"
  kicker="Plainly"
  description="Skipper is a free iPhone app that narrates your drive: GPS-triggered audio stories, grounded in cited sources, covering Lake Tahoe. What it does, where it works, and what it costs."
  updated="August 20, 2026"
>
  <p>
    Skipper is a <strong>narrated audio driving tour for iPhone</strong>. You mount your phone, start
    it, and drive. As you come near somewhere with a story, a corny old tour guide tells you what
    happened there, timed so the tale about the bay lands while you can still see the bay. There is
    nothing to look at and nothing to tap.
  </p>

  <h2>Where does it work right now?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Lake Tahoe</strong>, and nowhere else yet. There are hundreds of narrated stops around
    the basin. Every one is researched and recorded for a specific place, which is the reason
    coverage grows slowly and the reason it is worth having at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Outside Tahoe the app will tell you honestly that it doesn't know those roads. That is the
    designed behaviour, not a failure. More regions are the plan; there is no date to promise, and we
    would rather ship one place done properly than a nationwide map of nothing much.
  </p>

  <h2>What does it cost?</h2>
  <p>
    The app is <strong>free</strong>, and so is planning. You can talk to the Skipper about where
    you're going as long as you like, and change your mind as often as you like, without spending
    anything. <strong>Building</strong> a drive spends one of a set of free credits, because building
    one does real work. There is <strong>no subscription</strong>, there are no ads, and there is
    nothing to buy inside the app today.
  </p>

  <h2>Do I need an account?</h2>
  <p>
    Not to listen, and not to plan. With no account at all you can hear the Skipper, tell him where
    you'd like to drive, and hear a real clip from the route he comes back with. You only need a free
    account at the last step, to build and save the drive, because a saved drive belongs to somebody.
  </p>

  <h2>Does it work without a phone signal?</h2>
  <p>
    Yes, if you download a drive first. Mountain roads have real dead zones, so a downloaded drive
    plays entirely from your phone with no bars required.
  </p>

  <h2>Where do the facts come from?</h2>
  <p>
    Public, citable sources, principally
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>, reused under
    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, with
    places identified through <a href="https://www.wikidata.org/" rel="noopener">Wikidata</a>. Every
    stop carries its own source and licence, one tap away in the app, and the full list lives under
    Settings.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where the record is thin, the Skipper says so and lets the view do the talking. A guide who
    doesn't know is better than a guide who invents, and that rule is enforced in the pipeline rather
    than left to good intentions: a place without enough sourced material is downgraded to scenery
    instead of being given a story it hasn't earned.
  </p>

  <h2>Is it safe to use while driving?</h2>
  <p>
    It is built to be heard, not watched: audio-first, lock-screen controls, and it works from a mount
    or over Bluetooth. That said, the honest answer is the same as for anything else in a car. Mount
    your phone, start the drive, and keep your eyes where they belong. No story is worth it.
  </p>

  <h2>What do I need?</h2>
  {
    APP_STORE_URL ? (
      <p>
        An iPhone. Skipper is iOS-only, free, and on the
        <a href={APP_STORE_URL}>App Store</a>, in the US first. It uses your location only while
        you're on a drive, to time the stories, never in the background. Details are in the
        <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        An iPhone. Skipper is iOS-only, and it isn't in the App Store yet. This page and the
        <a href="/">front door</a> are where it lives until it is. When it ships it'll be a US release
        first. It uses your location only while you're on a drive, to time the stories, never in the
        background. Details are in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <h2>Something not answered here?</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/support">Support</a> covers what to do when something isn't working. Anything else,
    email <a href="mailto:hello@skipper.fm">hello@skipper.fm</a> and the person who built it will
    read it.
  </p>
</Legal>
